  "live_records": {
    "q-84756": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84756",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A student who answers randomly has a $50%$ chance of correctly answering each question on a $12$-question test. What is the probability that the student selects exactly $10$ correct answers? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of correct answers that the student gets.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ questions\n\n- the probability of hitting each question is $p=50%=0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.5).$ We want to find the probability of getting $x=10$ correct answers.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{10})(0.5)^{10}(1-0.5)^{12-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{10!(12-10)!}(0.5)^{10}(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =66(0.5)^{10}(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.016\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
